# Security review is a mandatory pre-commit close-out

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L1 core rule.

## Use This When

- Keep this rule active for any work that produces COMMITTABLE code, and ALWAYS at the
  moment before a feature commit. Highest priority when the change touches auth, access
  control / RLS, SQL / migrations / RPCs, HTTP endpoints, user input handling, secrets /
  env, or third-party dependencies.

## Stop Here If

- Stop after the close-out obligation is clear; this is a leaf rule.

## Descend To

- Access posture: the `create-rls-policies.mdc` rules (RLS is the sole authority;
  fail-closed, private-by-default).

## The mandate

A vulnerability/security review is a mandatory feature close-out. It is easy to forget by
hand — this rule makes it non-optional.

1. **Before committing feature work, run the `/security-review` skill** over the pending
   branch diff. It reviews the staged/working changes for vulnerabilities (injection,
   auth/access bypass, RLS gaps, secret leakage, unsafe deserialization, SSRF, missing
   authz on endpoints, service-role on a user path, …). Treat a confirmed finding as a
   blocker: fix it (or surface it as an explicit, justified decision) BEFORE the commit.
   Because skills run in the MAIN conversation, this step is the COORDINATOR's — a
   sub-agent cannot invoke it.

2. **Sub-agents do an inline security self-check + FLAG.** A feature/implementer sub-agent
   cannot run the skill, so before it reports done it self-reviews what it landed against
   the access/RLS rules and explicitly FLAGS any
   security-sensitive surface it touched (a new endpoint, an RLS policy, a SECURITY DEFINER
   function, raw SQL, an input parser, a secret/env read, a dependency bump) in its report,
   so the coordinator runs `/security-review` on the full diff before committing.

3. **DB migration → also run the Supabase advisors.** If the change includes a Postgres
   migration, the diff-level `/security-review` is NOT enough — run the live-DB advisors
   (`mcp__supabase-self-hosted__get_advisors`, security + performance) after applying it and
   fix new WARN/ERROR (missing RLS, anon/authenticated-exposed `security definer` functions,
   mutable `search_path`, public-bucket listing, GraphQL exposure). See `create-migration.mdc`'s
   "Post-apply" close-out. Don't let them accumulate.

4. **Exemptions.** A change with NO security surface (pure copy, a comment, a docs
   tweak) is exempt — say so. Never skip the review for anything touching the
   surfaces in "Use This When".

## Rationale

Security is the review with the most asymmetric cost: a single un-reviewed
auth/RLS/endpoint change can ship a real vulnerability, while the review itself is the
manual step most easily dropped under time pressure. Wiring the `/security-review` skill
into the pre-commit close-out makes coverage a property of the process, not of
remembering.
